the Iranian regime is clearly scared by  the scale of these protests which are  spreading around the country to every  section of society  groups of lawyers in Tehran the latest  to take to the streets they're chanting  women life and Freedom the signature of  these demonstrations  but there are signs of differences  emerging within the political Elite Ali  larajani an establishment figure and  presidential candidate in an interview  with Iranian media suggesting the  Crackdown may be counterproductive his  statements diverge from the hard line of  Ayatollah Ali kamini the supreme leader  what we can see beyond the surface is  precisely the need for the for the  regime to find a way to save face but at  the same time  um you know  um save itself as well and survive the  challenges that are coming from this  protest movement  the demonstrations began more than four  weeks ago following the death in custody  of this 22 year old Iranian Kurdish  woman Masa amini she'd been arrested by  the morality police for allegedly not  covering her hair properly  [Applause]  the anger at her death has turned into a  protest movement going far beyond the  hijack which women are now burning as an  act of defiance  but the Crackdown in many places has  been predictably brutal videos uploaded  to social media show Security Forces  arresting and beating protesters as they  try to restore order  places it seems they're failing with the  unrest continuing Into the Night  this is the dash where clouds of tear  gas envelop the streets as they shout  anti-regime slogans but there are  reports the city is now under the  control of the protesters  and whilst Talk of the beginning of the  end of the Islamic Republic may be  premature these demonstrations are  unlikely to stop anytime soon and will  remain a significant challenge to the  regime's authority  Alex Rossi Sky News
 

